WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.700
You might not be scared of zombies on tv,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.360
but you should definitely be&nbsp;
scared of these fungi in real life.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:13.020
For those expecting to see a one-to-one recreation&nbsp;
of Naughty Dog and Sony's 2013 PlayStation 3 game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.720
"The Last of Us," in the 2023 adaptation by HBO,&nbsp;
the opening likely came as quite the surprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.960
Instead of opening with Sarah giving Joel his&nbsp;
birthday present, we instead jump back to 1968,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.960
where we see an epidemiologist named Dr. Neuman&nbsp;
warn the world of a fungus called cordyceps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.500
which can take over the minds of&nbsp;
ants and bend them to their will.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.160
"The fungus starts to direct the&nbsp;
ant's behavior telling it where to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.040
what to do like a puppeteer with a marionette."

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.120
In the world of "The Last of Us," which&nbsp;
although grounded for a zombie show and game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.740
is still full of impossible creatures and&nbsp;
abominations, one might be forgiven for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.460
assuming that the cordyceps fungus&nbsp;
is a fictional creation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.340
we regret to inform you that the cordyceps fungus&nbsp;
is real, and it's just as scary as it sounds.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.040
Much like the scientist discussed in the premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:08.040
there are types of fungi that feed on the living.&nbsp;
One such fungus is Ophiocordyceps unilateralis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.380
which National Geographic reported on in 2019.&nbsp;
The fungus floods ants' brains with chemicals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.400
takes over their muscles, and controls them&nbsp;
to move to the perfect height and temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.480
for the fungus to grow, then spreads&nbsp;
its spores to control even more ants.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.400
There are a couple of differences between how&nbsp;
this fungus works and how it works in humans in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.580
"The Last of Us," however. For starters,&nbsp;
although it affects the ants' brains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.440
it doesn't actually invade them, as scientists&nbsp;
found no fungal cells within the ants' brains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.120
That means that the fungal outgrowths found&nbsp;
in Infected such as Clickers in the show and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.860
games wouldn't be possible with this fungus,&nbsp;
even if it were somehow able to infect humans.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.540
However, there is another fungus that&nbsp;
does create a scarily similar outgrowth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.000
from the head. According to National Geographic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.640
the fungus Ophiocordyceps sinensis can form&nbsp;
a growth from the heads of moth caterpillars.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.720
While you couldn't pay us enough to go anywhere&nbsp;
near those things, the infected caterpillars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.160
are actually used in traditional Tibetan and&nbsp;
Chinese medicine to cure all sorts of diseases.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.500
Not all types of cordyceps are malignant toward&nbsp;
their hosts, as species that take root in Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.080
cicadas may help them process nutrients from sap.&nbsp;
Imagine an alternate universe to "The Last of Us"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.240
where instead of causing an Infected horde, the&nbsp;
cordyceps fungus becomes a popular snack to boost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.340
humans' digestion! But these aren't the only&nbsp;
fungi that can take over their hosts' minds.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.960
According to National Geographic, the&nbsp;
Entomophthora muscae, translated from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.000
Greek as "insect destroyer of the fly," is&nbsp;
able to take over flies' actions and cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.560
them to fly to the perfect height for spore&nbsp;
dispersal, which it does before then striking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.300
a "death pose," the perfect angle for the fungus&nbsp;
to grow from its corpse and spread more spores.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:51.840
There is also the Massospora cicadina, which&nbsp;
drugs its cicada hosts so much that it causes its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.820
abdomen to partially fall off. The rest is similar&nbsp;
to what other fungi do to their hosts: move it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.260
an ideal climate for spreading spores, kill it,&nbsp;
then take over its body and do it all over again.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.580
The one thing consistent in all of these&nbsp;
cases, however, is that infection occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.740
exclusively through airborne spore dispersal,&nbsp;
an element that was present in the games but was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.760
removed from the HBO show. Although "The&nbsp;
Last of Us" certainly did its homework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.920
the part about infecting its hosts by biting them&nbsp;
appears to be purely the work of zombie fiction.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.660
"The Last of Us" airs on HBO and&nbsp;
HBO Max Sundays at 9 p.m. ET.

